<BlogArticle
	title="How to Scan and Crop a PAN Card Signature on a White Background"
	description="Learn how to scan or photograph a PAN signature with a white background, crop it cleanly, and prepare a readable file for the current PAN application."
	canonicalUrl="https://nsdlphotoresize.com/blogs/scan-crop-pan-signature/"
	heading="How to scan and crop a signature for a PAN card with a white background"
	intro="A well-prepared signature image starts with a clear capture of the original signature. Even lighting, a plain white background, and a careful crop usually matter more than aggressive editing."
>
	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Sign and capture on clean paper</h2>
		<ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
			<li>Use clean, uncreased white paper and sign in the manner and ink color requested by the current application.</li>
			<li>Use a flatbed scanner if available. Place the paper flat and scan in color or grayscale at a resolution sufficient to keep thin strokes clear.</li>
			<li>If using a phone camera, put the paper on a flat surface in bright, even light. Hold the phone parallel to the page and avoid your hand or phone casting a shadow.</li>
			<li>Keep the whole signature, including the first and last strokes, within the captured frame.</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Crop without damaging the signature</h2>
		<p>
			Straighten a tilted scan, then crop away unused paper while
			leaving a narrow white margin around the signature. Do not crop
			too tightly: the pen may extend farther than it first appears,
			and a little space makes the result easier to review. Keep the
			aspect ratio requested by the current portal; do not distort the
			signature to fill a rectangular box.
		</p>
		<p>
			Use a <a class="text-link" href="/#photo-tool">PAN card signature resizer</a>
			to adjust the crop and export a suitable JPEG. Confirm the
			dimensions and size from the current application rather than
			assuming an old NSDL or UTIITSL preset is still valid.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Keep the background white and the ink visible</h2>
		<p>
			Avoid colored paper, patterned surfaces, desk edges, and
			high-contrast shadows. If the paper appears gray because of
			uneven lighting, make a better capture before adjusting levels.
			Overexposure can erase faint ink; over-darkening can turn the
			background gray and thicken the strokes. Review the final image
			against a white screen and zoom in to check that every mark is
			intact.
		</p>
		<p>
			Do not use a filter that removes specks by also removing dots or
			thin lines from the handwritten signature. The goal is a faithful,
			legible image of the signed paper—not a digitally recreated
			signature.
		</p>
	</section>

	<p>
		When the file is ready, check that it opens, uses an accepted format,
		and meets the current application’s limit. Resizing does not
		guarantee acceptance and cannot replace the original signature
		requirements.
	</p>
</BlogArticle>
